hi my name is Justin Pearson I'm a  graduate student in electrical and  computer engineering at University of  California at Santa Barbara and I taught  a class in intro to programming for  engineers and all sorts of different  engineers take this class mechanical or  chemical electrical and they would  always stumble we taught this class in  MATLAB they would always stumble at  certain points in the class because they  had to contort their thinking into a  form that MATLAB could understand so for  instance with ordinary differential  equations you can't just tell MATLAB to  solve the differential equation you have  to boil down the nth order equation into  a vector first-order equation you have  to make a separate M file that encodes  the right-hand side you have to call  this specially named function that has  some weird numbers in it and it's really  confusing for students because it  doesn't look like anything like what  they learned in their math classes so I  took one day I told them you know there  are other programming languages out  there and I showed them in the Wolfram  language how you can solve a  differential equation with a single line  and then you can plot out the the  solution to that equation and look at  and get all sorts of physical insight  from it that you wouldn't have if you  just got a pile of numbers from MATLAB  and I had some friends in the audience  who said that when they sauce to  students reactions to this the students  were awestruck by the the ability of the  Wolfram language to solve a really  complicated math problem without having  to go through the rigmarole that they  would have had to do in MATLAB they came  up to me afterwards after this  presentation and some of them had their  laptops in hand and they were logged on  to the Wolfram programming lab getting  started for the first time and they were  just so thrilled that instantly they  could in their browser get started  learning this new language and making  their lives and engineering careers a  lot easier
 

